{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"04","chapter_title":"Course Completion and Transfer","title":"AI Use Disclosure Portfolio","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting ai use disclosure portfolio to resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/08/03"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_8_4_ai_use_disclosure_portfolio_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_8_4_ai_use_disclosure_portfolio_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_8_4_ai_use_disclosure_portfolio_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_8_4_ai_use_disclosure_portfolio_calibration"]},"content":"# AI Use Disclosure Portfolio\n\n![AI Use Disclosure Portfolio textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/08-04-ai-use-disclosure-portfolio.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAI tools will happily rewrite your resume bullet to sound impressive, and that is exactly the danger. A chatbot does not know what you actually did; it optimizes for words that sound strong. Left unchecked, it turns \"tracked a small club budget\" into \"spearheaded financial operations,\" a phrase you cannot defend the moment an interviewer asks a follow-up question. The skill this section teaches is not avoiding AI, it is using it with a visible boundary: accept what is true, reject what inflates, and disclose what you did.\n\nAn AI use disclosure portfolio pairs each AI-assisted artifact with an honest note about what the tool proposed, what you kept, and what you refused and why. This is becoming a professional norm: employers and admissions offices increasingly ask how you used AI, and a clear disclosure signals integrity rather than concealment. The portfolio proves you can collaborate with a tool without letting it write claims you cannot stand behind.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nPriya tracked her campus club's \\$600 snack budget in a spreadsheet and caught a 12% overspend. Her first resume bullet reads, \"Helped with the club budget.\" She asks an AI tool to make it stronger, and it returns, \"Spearheaded financial operations and led analytics for a campus organization.\"\n\nThe new sentence is more impressive and less true. Priya did not lead operations or run analytics; she tracked a small budget with a `SUM` formula and flagged a problem. If she keeps the AI phrasing, the first sharp interview question collapses it. The disclosure portfolio is where she records that she took the verb upgrade, rejected the inflation, and kept the bullet defensible.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAI use disclosure rests on a clear line between two kinds of help. Acceptable AI help improves how you express something you genuinely did: a stronger verb, tighter phrasing, a fixed grammar slip. Unacceptable AI help invents or inflates the underlying claim: a title you did not hold, a scope you did not own, a number you cannot verify. The disclosure note is the record that you policed that line.\n\nA defensible resume bullet has three properties the AI cannot supply on its own:\n\n- **Truthful.** Every claim matches what happened. \"Tracked a \\$600 budget\" is true; \"led financial operations\" is not.\n- **Role-fit.** The verb matches your actual scope. \"Tracked\" and \"flagged\" fit a member tracking a small budget; \"spearheaded\" implies authority you did not have.\n- **Verifiable.** The number traces to a real artifact. The 12% overspend lives in the spreadsheet and can be reproduced with `=SUM(B2:B13)`.\n\nThe disclosure note then makes the collaboration transparent: it states which suggestion you accepted, which you rejected, and the reason. The reason is the important part. \"Rejected because it overstates a \\$600 task I cannot defend\" shows judgment; \"rejected because I did not like it\" shows nothing. Disclosure is not an apology for using AI; it is evidence that you supervised it.\n\n<inline-check question=\"AI rewrites your bullet to Spearheaded financial operations for a campus organization. You actually tracked a $600 club budget. Keep it?\" explanation=\"Accept AI help with expression, reject AI help with facts. Spearheaded financial operations fails the truth and role-fit tests and collapses the moment an interviewer asks a follow-up; the truthful, verifiable bullet is the one you can defend.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Yes, it sounds more impressive and recruiters like strong verbs.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No, it inflates a $600 task you cannot defend in an interview.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, as long as you do not mention you used AI.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: A Truthful Before/After Resume Bullet with an AI-Use Note\n\nA student tracked a campus club's \\$600 snack budget in a spreadsheet and flagged a 12% overspend. They want one strong resume bullet and an honest AI-use note.\n\n**Before (vague, written alone).**\n\n> Helped with the club budget.\n\n**AI proposal (one bounded critique).** The student prompts: \"Make this resume bullet more specific and action-oriented.\" The AI returns:\n\n> Spearheaded financial operations and led analytics for a campus organization.\n\n**Decision (judged against the truth).** The role was tracking a small budget, not leading operations. The student rejects \"spearheaded financial operations\" as inflated and keeps the specific, true result.\n\n**After (truthful, role-fit, defensible in an interview).**\n\n> Tracked a \\$600 club budget in a spreadsheet using `=SUM(B2:B13)`, flagged a 12% overspend, and proposed a cut that kept the term in budget.\n\n**AI-use note (the disclosure).**\n\n> Used an AI tool for one wording pass on this bullet. Accepted the suggestion to start with a strong verb. Rejected \"spearheaded financial operations\" and \"led analytics\" because they overstate a \\$600 tracking task I cannot defend. Final numbers are mine and verifiable in the spreadsheet.\n\nThe portfolio does not hide the AI use; it shows the boundary. A reviewer can see exactly what was proposed, what was kept, and why one suggestion was refused.\n\nWork the accept and reject boundary as a procedure. Predict each step before revealing it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run one bounded AI pass on a resume bullet\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Before you ask AI to improve a bullet, what should you write down first?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The most impressive verb you can think of\",\"The plain, truthful version of what you actually did\",\"Three job titles you hope to hold someday\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Anchor on the truth first: tracked a $600 club budget and caught a 12% overspend. Every later edit gets measured against reality, not ambition.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Invite one bounded AI pass, then run the truth check: keep the verb upgrade that states what you did more clearly.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Run the defense check: reject Spearheaded financial operations because it claims a role you did not hold and would collapse on a follow-up question.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Write the disclosure note: tool used, suggestion accepted, suggestion rejected and why, ending with final numbers are mine and verifiable with =SUM(B2:B13).\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nNow order the boundary-setting moves:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Write the plain, truthful version of what you did\",\"Run one bounded AI pass for wording\",\"Apply the truth check, keeping only claims that happened\",\"Apply the defense check against an interview follow-up\",\"Record the disclosure note: accepted, rejected, and the reason\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThese four concepts anchor the section; trace how they connect:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer\",\"ai_use_disclosure\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nAccept AI help with expression; reject AI help with facts. Before keeping any AI-suggested wording, run the interview test:\n\n1. **Truth check.** Is every claim in the suggestion something that actually happened? If not, cut it regardless of how good it sounds.\n2. **Defense check.** If an interviewer said \"tell me more about that,\" could you, without backpedaling? \"Spearheaded operations\" fails because the next sentence is \"well, it was a \\$600 budget.\"\n3. **Disclosure check.** Have you written down what you accepted, what you rejected, and why? If the boundary is not on paper, you have not really set one.\n\nIf a suggestion survives truth and defense, keep it and disclose it. If it fails either, reject it and record the reason.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is thinking disclosure means confessing wrongdoing. Using AI to sharpen a verb is normal and fine; the disclosure simply documents a healthy boundary. Hiding the use is what creates suspicion, not the use itself.\n\nThe second misreading is judging a bullet by how impressive it sounds instead of whether you can defend it. \"Spearheaded financial operations\" reads better than \"tracked a \\$600 budget\" right up to the interview, where the inflated version becomes a liability and the truthful one becomes a story you can tell confidently.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nStart with the truthful, unpolished version of what you did, in plain words: \"tracked a small club budget and caught an overspend.\" Anchoring on the truth first means every later edit gets measured against reality instead of against ambition.\n\nNow invite one bounded AI pass and read the result against the three properties. The verb upgrade (\"tracked\" stated cleanly, the result foregrounded) passes truth, role-fit, and verifiability, so keep it. The title inflation (\"spearheaded financial operations\") fails role-fit because you held no such role, so reject it. Pin the verifiable number to its source by naming the formula, `=SUM(B2:B13)`, so a reader knows the 12% is real.\n\nFinally, write the disclosure note as a short paragraph: tool used, suggestion accepted, suggestion rejected, and the reason for the rejection, ending with \"final numbers are mine and verifiable.\" The walkthrough output is a before bullet, an after bullet, and a disclosure note a reviewer can read as a single honest record.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, take one of your own real activities and run it through the same accept/reject pass, writing the disclosure note. A strong reader does not just nod at Priya's example; they can identify, in their own resume, where an AI suggestion would cross from expression into invention.\n\nA strong reader of the AI use disclosure portfolio can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which exact phrase did the AI propose, and was it true?\n2. What did you accept, and does it pass the interview defense test?\n3. What did you reject, and is the reason about truth and role-fit rather than taste?\n4. Where does the verifiable number live, and can a reader reproduce it?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe portfolio is strong when each AI-assisted bullet is truthful, role-fit, and verifiable, and the disclosure note names a specific accepted and a specific rejected suggestion with reasons. The standard is not \"I used AI responsibly\" as a slogan; it is a paired before/after plus a note that shows the boundary in action.\n\nA useful closing note would read: \"I accepted the AI suggestion to lead with a strong verb because it states what I did more clearly. I rejected 'spearheaded financial operations' and 'led analytics' because they claim authority and scope I did not have on a \\$600 tracking task and could not defend in an interview. The 12% overspend is reproducible in my spreadsheet with `=SUM(B2:B13)`.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the disclosure from a resume bullet to a different artifact: an AI-assisted cover letter, or AI-generated starter code in your portfolio site. The accept/reject logic holds, but the inflation looks different. In a cover letter, AI may assert enthusiasm or fit you have not earned; in code, it may add a feature you did not test or a comment claiming functionality that does not exist. Writing an accurate disclosure for code you only partly verified, including which lines you tested and which you cannot vouch for, is the test of whether you understand disclosure as ongoing supervision rather than a one-line resume footnote.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/08-04-ai-use-disclosure-portfolio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"08.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note for CAT 100 08.04. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_ai_use_disclosure_portfolio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the resume before-after revision with truthful evidence, role fit, and AI-use note records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_4_ai_use_disclosure_portfolio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_use_disclosure_portfolio","concept_ids":["ai_use_disclosure","cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer"],"stem":"Your real role was tracking a student club's $600 snack budget in a spreadsheet. An AI tool rewrites your resume bullet as 'Led financial operations for a campus organization.' What should you do?","options":["Keep it; it sounds more impressive and the AI suggested it.","Reject the inflated verb and write 'Tracked a $600 club budget in a spreadsheet, flagging a 12% overspend' which you can defend in an interview.","Keep it but add 'approximately' in front.","Keep both versions on the resume."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A resume claim must be true and defensible. 'Led financial operations' overstates a $600 tracking task; the honest, specific bullet is stronger and verifiable.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_4_ai_inflated_claim","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_4_ai_use_disclosure_portfolio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"You used an AI tool to tighten the wording of three resume bullets. What is the purpose of the AI-use note in this portfolio?","options":["To confess that you cheated on the resume.","To document the boundary: what the AI proposed, which suggestions you accepted, and which you rejected, so the work stays honest and reviewable.","To hide the AI use behind a generic 'I worked hard' statement.","To take credit for the AI's writing as your own."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disclosure records a bounded, judged use of AI. It is not a confession; it shows you stayed in control of the truthful content.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_4_disclosure_is_confession","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_4_ai_use_disclosure_portfolio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"A job posting lists 'data visualization' and 'SQL'. You built a bar chart in Excel but have never used SQL. What is the truthful, role-fit move?","options":["Paste both keywords into the resume so the automated screener accepts it.","Add a bullet citing the Excel bar chart for data visualization, and leave SQL off because you have no evidence for it.","List SQL as a skill since it is 'related' to spreadsheets.","Ask the AI to write SQL experience for you."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Match the role with true evidence. Cite the chart you actually built; do not claim a skill you cannot demonstrate just to match a keyword.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_4_keyword_stuffing","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_8_4_ai_use_disclosure_portfolio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which before/after record best documents a truthful, AI-supported resume revision?","options":["'Made my resume better with AI.'","'Before: \"Did data stuff.\" After: \"Built an Excel bar chart of 6 months of club attendance.\" AI proposed \"spearheaded analytics\"; rejected as inflated; kept the chart-based bullet.'","'AI fixed my resume.'","'Resume is final.'"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A verifiable record shows the before line, the after line, the AI proposal, and the accept/reject decision with a reason, so a reviewer can audit honesty.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_8_4_ai_inflated_claim","difficulty":"medium"}]}}